text:
Both problems are real.

**C1: confirmed, blocking.** In `creditOrders` (`src/loyalty.ts:17`), `orders.map` starts every `addPoints` call at once. Each call runs until its first `await store.get(key)` (line 11), so every call for the same customer reads the balance before any of them writes it. Each then writes its own `balance + points` (line 12), and the last write wins. With the test's in-memory store, two 1000-cent orders for ana starting at 3 points both read 3 and both write 13, so ana ends with 13 instead of 23. `Store` (`src/store.ts:2-5`) has only `get` and `set`, with no atomic increment that could prevent this. Customers silently lose points, so the change must not merge as is.

**C2: confirmed, should_fix.** The only test that credits a batch (`src/loyalty.test.ts:23-26`) gives ana and bo one order each, so the concurrent calls never share a key and the race in C1 never runs. The suggested test is right: ana starts at 3 points, and two 1000-cent orders add 10 points each, so she should end with 23. Against the current code it would fail with 13.

